 stretches from the south bank of the Yoshino River in the town of Yoshino central Nara Prefecture, Japan, to the Ōmine Mountains, stretching for about eight kilometers from north-to-south, or the broader name of the area dotted with shrines and temples, centered around Kinpusen-ji Temple. It has long been known as a famous flower spot, especially for its cherry blossoms and is a popular tourist destination. It was designated a National Place of Scenic Beauty and National Historic Site in 1924 and became part of Yoshino-Kumano National Park in 1936. In 2004, Mount Yoshino was designated as part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site under the name Sacred Sites and Pilgrimage Routes in the Kii Mountain Range. In 1990, it was selected as one of Japan''s 100 Best Cherry Blossom Spots.", "sections": [{"index": "1", "level": 1, "title": "Mount Yoshino and Cherry Blossoms", "content": ""}, {"index": "2", "level": 1, "title": "Mount Yoshino as a sacred mountain", "content": ""}, {"index": "3", "level": 1, "title": "Hiking", "content": ""}, {"index": "4", "level": 1, "title": "Gallery", "content": ""}, {"index": "5", "level": 1, "title": "See also", "content": ""}, {"index": "6", "level": 1, "title": "Notes", "content": ""}, {"index": "7", "level": 1, "title": "External links", "content": ""}], "citations": [{"ref": "wikipedia", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Yoshino", "text": "Wikipedia"}], "extract_text": "Mount Yoshino  is the general name for the mountain ridge that stretches from the south bank of the Yoshino River in the town of Yoshino central Nara Prefecture, Japan, to the Ōmine Mountains, stretching for about eight kilometers from north-to-south, or the broader name of the area dotted with shrines and temples, centered around Kinpusen-ji Temple. It has long been known as a famous flower spot, especially for its cherry blossoms and is a popular tourist destination. It was designated a National Place of Scenic Beauty and National Historic Site in 1924 and became part of Yoshino-Kumano National Park in 1936. In 2004, Mount Yoshino was designated as part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site under the name Sacred Sites and Pilgrimage Routes in the Kii Mountain Range. In 1990, it was selected as one of Japan''s 100 Best Cherry Blossom Spots."}, "popularity": 0.7, "visual_tags": [{"tags": ["mountain", "temple", "japan", "spring_greenery", "hiking_trail", "panoramic", "exterior", "crowded", "empty", "blooming_flowers", "unknown_country"], "season": "unknown", "evidence": {}, "confidence": 0.5, "tag_source": "gpt_4o_mini_image_history"}], "name_primary": "吉野山", "viewpoint_id": 70399, "category_norm": "attraction", "name_variants": {"name:en": "Mount Yoshino", "name:fr": "Mont Yoshino", "name:ja-Hira": "よしのやま", "name:ja-Latn": "Yoshino-yama"}, "historical_summary": "Mount Yoshino  is the general name for the mountain ridge that stretches from the south bank of the Yoshino River in the town of Yoshino central Nara Prefecture, Japan, to the Ōmine Mountains, stretching for about eight kilometers from north-to-south, or the broader name of the area dotted with shrines and temples, centered around Kinpusen-ji Temple. It has long been known as a famous flower spot, especially for its cherry blossoms and is a popular tourist destination. It was designated a National Place of Scenic Beauty and National Historic Site in 1924 and became part of Yoshino-Kumano National Park in 1936. In 2004, Mount Yoshino was designated as part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site under the name Sacred Sites and Pilgrimage Routes in the Kii Mountain Range. In 1990, it was selected as one of Japan''s 100 Best Cherry Blossom Spots.", "historical_evidence": [{"text": "Mount Yoshino  is the general name for the mountain ridge that stretches from the south bank of the Yoshino River in the town of Yoshino central Nara Prefecture, Japan, to the Ōmine Mountains, stretch...", "source": "wikipedia", "reference": "吉野山"}, {"text": "Wikipedia", "source": "wikipedia_citation", "re